  <h1>J A S P E R S   F L I G H T</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>He floated to the floor and landed on his feet, and the room looked exactly the same as it had before, and his body felt exactly the same, and he understood that this was the moment everything was going to be different.</p>
<p>He did not tell anyone. Who would he tell? What would he say? <em>I can fly. Not in my dreams. In my life.</em> He could already hear the words people would use: psychotic break, hallucination, delusion. He was seventeen, he was apparently mentally ill, and the world did not have a category for what he was.</p>
<p>So he kept it to himself. He learned to control it — learned that it came when he stopped fighting, when he let the fear flow through him and out the other side. He learned to rise and fall, to drift across his room, to hover by the window and watch the street below like a bird watching worms.</p>
<p>He did not go outside. He was not stupid. He was not ready to become a headline.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>The summer he turned eighteen, his mother found out.</p>
<p>She came home early from a date — she'd started dating again, a man named Paul who sold furniture and laughed too loudly — and she came upstairs to tell him something about dinner and she found him.</p>
<p>He was in the kitchen. He'd been practicing, trying to build up the muscle of it, the will of it, and he hadn't heard her key in the lock. He was two feet off the ground, hovering near the ceiling with a glass of water in his hand, and when she walked in he was simply there, hanging in the air like something that had always belonged there.</p>
<p>The glass fell. It shattered against the floor. Neither of them moved for a long moment.</p>
<p>"Jasper," she said. Her voice was very quiet. "What are you doing."</p>
<p>He could have lied. He could have said he'd been jumping, that he'd somehow gotten caught on something, that it was a trick of the light. He was eighteen and he was tired of lies, tired of the separate architecture of his life, tired of being the only one who knew the true shape of things.</p>
<p>"I'm flying," he said.</p>
<p>"That's not possible."</p>
<p>"I know," he said. "I know it isn't."</p>
<p>He descended slowly, the way he'd learned to do — not falling but settling, like a leaf or a snowflake, something that had always known how to reach the ground without fear. His bare feet touched the tile. The shattered glass crunched beneath them.</p>
<p>She was staring at him like he'd become something else, something her eyes couldn't reconcile with the person she thought she knew. And maybe he was. Maybe he had been becoming this all along, and the dreams had been the slow work of it, the long preparation for a life that didn't obey the usual rules.</p>
<p>"How long," she said.</p>
<p>"Since I was seven."</p>
<p>Seventeen years of secrets, and he said it in four words, and she said nothing, and the kitchen filled with the weight of everything that had gone unsaid.</p>
<p>She sat down on one of the kitchen chairs. She put her face in her hands. He stood in the broken glass and waited, because he didn't know what else to do.</p>
<p>"I thought it was my fault," she said, eventually, muffled by her hands. "I thought I did something wrong, when you were little. I thought — you were always somewhere else. Even when you were right in front of me. You were always somewhere far away."</p>
<p>"I was flying," he said. "In my head. I was flying."</p>
<p>She looked up at him, and her eyes were red, and her face was the same face it had always been, and he understood that this was the truest thing they'd ever shared with each other.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>He left home at nineteen. Not because of the flying — because of the usual things, the ordinary forces that push young men out into the world. College applications and financial aid and the desire to be somewhere that didn't contain his childhood bedroom and all its ghosts.</p>
<p>He went to a school four hundred miles away, far enough that the familiar sky didn't look down on him, and he lived in a dorm room and studied architecture and told no one about what he could do. He was good at pretending. He'd had years of practice.</p>
<p>But at night, when the campus was quiet, he would rise from his bed and drift to the window and open it and slip out into the air. He learned the city from above — the angles of rooftops, the cathedral spires, the parks that held their darkness like cupped water. He learned how to move without sound, how to let the wind carry him, how to fold himself into updrafts and ride them like a raptor.</p>
<p>He never flew high. He never flew far. He was still afraid, still that small boy standing at the edge of a roof, still waiting for the world to prove him wrong.</p>
<p>But he flew.</p>
<p>···</p>
<p>He was twenty-three when he finally flew in front of someone else on purpose.</p>
<p>Her name was Linh. She was a graduate student in the physics department who worked nights at the campus library, and he had been finding excuses to be there for weeks — not because he wanted to talk to her but because he wanted to be near someone who didn't know. She was quiet and serious and she wore glasses that she was always pushing up her nose, and when she smiled it was like watching a door open in a wall that had been solid for years.</p>
<p>One night in December, he came in late and she was the only one there, and she looked up from her books and said, "You come here a lot."</p>
<p>"I like the quiet."</p>
<p>"The library is closing in twenty minutes."</p>
<p>"I know."</p>
<p>She closed her book. She looked at him the way she looked at everything — carefully, like she was cataloging details for later. "What's your name?"</p>
<p>"Jasper."</p>
<p>"I'm Linh."</p>
<p>"I know."</p>
<p>She smiled. "That's not creepy at all."</p>
<p>"I'm not — I'm not trying to be creepy. I just think you're interesting. And I don't say that to very many people."</p>
<p>She studied him for a moment, and then she said, "Do you want to get coffee? There's an all-night place on Maple Street."</p>
<p>They walked to the coffee shop. They talked about nothing — her research, his classes, the winter that was coming on too fast. At two in the morning they walked back across the empty campus, and he was telling her something about his father's absence, a story he'd never told anyone, and the words came out and kept coming out, and she listened, and somewhere in the middle of it she said, "You look like you're floating."</p>
<p>He stopped walking. "What?"</p>
<p>"You walk like you're not quite touching the ground. It's — I don't know. It's interesting. You look like you're lighter than you should be."</p>
<p>He looked down at his feet. They were touching the pavement. They had always been touching the pavement. But she had seen something, some quality of movement that his body had learned and his mind had forgotten, the way a person who's been swimming for years still moves differently on land, carrying the memory of the water in their muscles.</p>
<p>"Linh," he said. "I need to show you something."</p>
<p>Not because he loved her — he barely knew her. Not because he trusted her — he'd known her for weeks. But because he was twenty-three and he was tired of the separate life, the double architecture, the self that was real and the self that performed, and she was the first person who had looked at him and seen something true even when she couldn't have known what it was.</p>
<p>He stepped into the empty lawn beside the science building and he rose. He rose the way he always did — not dramatically, not with a flourish, but slowly, like a diver ascending from the deep, and he kept rising until he was ten feet off the ground, fifteen, twenty, until the streetlights were below him and the sky was close and his body was horizontal, parallel to the earth, his arms at his sides and his face toward the stars.</p>
<p>Linh stood on the grass and looked up at him. She did not scream. She did not run. She put her hands in her coat pockets and she watched, and when he finally descended — slowly, carefully, giving her time to understand that he was still the same person she had been talking to moments before — she said:</p>
<p>"Well. That explains the walking."</p>
